152 :                 buf = resizeArray(buf);
153 :                 status = fill(buf, size, in);
154 :                 size = status[SIZE_KEY];
155 :                 lastRead = status[LAST_READ_KEY];
156 :             }
157 :             if (buf.length != size) {
158 :                 byte[] smallerBuf = new byte[size];
159 :                 System.arraycopy(buf, 0, smallerBuf, 0, size);
160 :                 buf = smallerBuf;
161 :             }
162 :         }
163 :         finally {
164 :             in.close();
165 :         }
166 :         return buf;
167 :     }"

The failing test `testCodec98NPE` calls the method `streamToBytes` from `Base64TestData` class. This method reads from an input stream and fills a buffer with the data read until the end of the stream is reached. 

The current bug report describes a `NullPointerException` occurring in `Base64.decode` due to specific conditions: when the buffer is null, the modulus is 3 from a previous entry, and `inAvail` is -1 because `Base64InputStream.read` reached EOF. This exception arises at line 581 of `Base64.decode` with the buffer still being null.

Looking at the `Base64InputStream` constructor, we can see that it calls another constructor with `false` as the second argument:
```java
60 :     public Base64InputStream(InputStream in) {
61 :         this(in, false);
62 :     }
```

The constructor that is actually invoked is:
```java
66 :     public Base64InputStream(InputStream in, boolean doEncode) {
67 :         this(in, doEncode, Base64.DEFAULT_LINE_LENGTH, Base64.DEFAULT_LINE_SEPARATOR);
68 :     }
```

The `Base64InputStream` constructor sets up the input stream for decoding base64 data. The issue described in the bug report could be related to how the input stream is handled or how the base64 decoding is performed in the `Base64InputStream` class.

To diagnose further, we may need to investigate the implementation details of the `Base64InputStream` class to understand how it handles the input stream and buffers the data for decoding.

